\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Features and limitations of the first dataset}
  \begin{itemize}
  \item It is adjusted to use with geocoded DHS data via the customised administrative divisions. Our R package \href{https://bitbucket.org/hansekbrand/dhsharmonisation/src/master/}{globallivingconditions} connects DHS data to these administrative divisions.
  \item It is available in two versions: with and without geometry data.
  \item The first published dataset only has data about forest coverage, no data about living conditions.
  \item It only contains data about changes in forest coverage, not changes in land use in general.
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Our second dataset: linking DHS data to deforestation data}
  \begin{itemize}
  \item Based on geocoded deforestation data for every fifth year from year 2000 up to 2020, and geocoded data on living conditions based on the DHS, construct a data set that includes an area average for household wealth index (IWI), GNI, and the change in forest coverage for the forthcoming five years.
  \item DHS is dated irregularly.
  \item Aggregating DHS measurements from different points in an area creates sampling noise.
  \item DHS adds a random offset to the coordinates, so some points end up in the wrong area.
  \item We estimate a trend of IWI development over time for all areas, and include the education of a fixed cohort to reduce sampling noise and improve the estimate.
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\fullframeimage{impute-based-on-education}

